    "title": "pandas.Timestamp.to_julian_date — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1386.gcb2086a1a4 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Timestamp.to_julian_date#\n\n- Timestamp.to_julian_date()#\n- Convert Timestamp to a Julian Date. This method returns the number of days as a float since 0 Julian date, which is noon January 1, 4713 BC. See also \n  - `Timestamp.toordinal`\n  - Return proleptic Gregorian ordinal.\n  - `Timestamp.timestamp`\n  - Return POSIX timestamp as float.\n  - `Timestamp`\n  - Represents a single timestamp.\n Examples >>> ts = pd.Timestamp('2020-03-14T15:32:52') >>> ts.to_julian_date() 2458923.147824074",
